{
  "id": "ep059",
  "duration": 44.574,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.13,
      "text": "Astronauts floating in orbit are still trapped by Earth's gravity.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "ISS after STS-119 in March 2009 1.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS_after_STS-119_in_March_2009_1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.13,
      "end": 10.5,
      "text": "In 1961, Soviet cosmonaut Gherman Titov reported the first case of space sickness.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The Soviet Union 1961 CPA 2606 stamp (Second Manned Orbital Space Flight. Major Titov and Vostok 2).jpg",
        "credit": "Post of the Soviet Union (I. L. Levin).",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Soviet_Union_1961_CPA_2606_stamp_(Second_Manned_Orbital_Space_Flight._Major_Titov_and_Vostok_2).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.133,
          "end": 10.5,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.5,
      "end": 15.52,
      "text": "This sickness occurs even though space is actually not a gravity-free vacuum.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Solar sys.jpg",
        "credit": "Harman Smith and Laura Generosa (nee Berwin), graphic artists and contractors to NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory.",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solar_sys.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.5,
          "end": 15.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 15.52,
      "end": 22.19,
      "text": "At the altitude of the space station, Earth's gravity is nearly ninety percent as strong as on the ground.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Earth vs Mars gravity at elevation.webp",
        "credit": "Wikideas1",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Earth_vs_Mars_gravity_at_elevation.webp"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 15.533,
          "end": 22.2,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 22.19,
      "end": 28.865,
      "text": "Normal gravity on Earth's surface pulls at approximately nine point eight meters per second squared.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 22.2,
          "end": 28.867,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 28.865,
      "end": 34.61,
      "text": "In orbit, gravity is still highly active, but spacecraft are in constant free fall.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Astronaut Nichole Ayers poses for a portrait inside the seven window cupola (iss072e978930).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Johnson Space Center",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astronaut_Nichole_Ayers_poses_for_a_portrait_inside_the_seven_window_cupola_(iss072e978930).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 28.867,
          "end": 32.867,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 32.867,
          "end": 34.6,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 34.61,
      "end": 40.655,
      "text": "High horizontal speed lets them continuously fall over the curving horizon of the Earth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Newton's cannonball cropped.png",
        "credit": "Isaac Newton",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newton%27s_cannonball_cropped.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 34.6,
          "end": 38.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 38.533,
          "end": 40.667,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 40.655,
      "end": 44.224,
      "text": "And because they never stop falling around Earth, it feels like",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "ISS after STS-119 in March 2009 1.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS_after_STS-119_in_March_2009_1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 40.667,
          "end": 44.567,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
